## Safe prerequisites
- Preserve the original artifact hash, package/version and decompilation limitations.
- Confirm the exact researcher-owned app, current Android build and dedicated device serial.
- Use synthetic accounts, resources and canaries only; no unrelated apps or destinations.
- Start with an unrooted ordinary attacker context. ADB shell, root and authorization-changing hooks do not establish ordinary-app access.

## Controlled validation
1. Derive the real component type/name and effective permissions from the manifest and active package; do not infer an authority/action from a class name.
2. Trace its actual inputs to sensitive operations, including in-code caller/role checks.
3. Use a researcher-owned unprivileged harness with only declared inputs and a harmless synthetic canary. Compare allowed and unauthorized callers without changing app authorization.
4. For PendingIntent candidates, instead review token distribution, effective mutability, explicit destination, fill-in fields and URI grants. Missing flags alone are not privilege-escalation proof.
5. Record a boundary crossing only if the unauthorized caller reaches a sensitive synthetic operation. Do not invoke deletion, installation, real account changes or arbitrary services merely because a component is exported.

## Evidence and conclusion
Record the exact entrypoint, attacker-controlled input, security boundary, sensitive sink, observed impact and commands/test-app source actually used. Attach redacted evidence plus successful authorized and rejected unauthorized controls. Distinguish unsupported observations, negative results and reproduced impact. No outcome or attacker capability is asserted by this plan.

